00:00:02.639 --> 00:00:04.070
So, we're now going to look at some of

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:05.749
the very basics that we need to

00:00:05.759 --> 00:00:08.870
understand about VHDL.

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:10.950
Um, in outline in this video, I'm going

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:13.669
to look at the basic types, identifiers,

00:00:13.679 --> 00:00:15.430
data types, and operators that we have

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:18.390
in VHDL. We're going to look at signals.

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:19.910
These are what we use to represent

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:22.710
electronic signals in a circuit. Um, I

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:24.630
will mention variables, but at this

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:26.710
beginner level, I advise that we only

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:29.189
use signals. We're going to use at the

00:00:29.199 --> 00:00:31.669
main design unit in VHDL which is the

00:00:31.679 --> 00:00:34.870
entity architecture construct going to

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:36.709
mention about using libraries and

00:00:36.719 --> 00:00:37.990
packages. We're not going to be creating

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:40.310
our own in this introductory course but

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:43.030
we will need to use the IEEE libraries

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:45.430
of for numerical types and for standard

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:48.069
logic and then I'm going to go through a

00:00:48.079 --> 00:00:50.790
simple design example uh using a data

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:52.470
flow approach. So remember that we've

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:54.549
got our three approaches data flow

00:00:54.559 --> 00:00:56.470
structural and behavioral. At the moment

00:00:56.480 --> 00:00:59.670
I'm only going to look at data flow.

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:01.189
So the first thing is we need to name

00:01:01.199 --> 00:01:03.750
various objects and we need identifiers.

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:05.350
They're used as the names of signals and

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:07.350
variables constants and the various

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:10.310
design units entities and architectures.

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:12.070
It's just a sequence of characters as

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:15.030
shown here. So it's either A to Z to 9

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:17.749
or an underscore. It's case insensitive.

00:01:17.759 --> 00:01:19.670
So it doesn't differentiate uppercase

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:22.310
and lowerase in the language itself. You

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:24.310
can use uppercase or lower case if you

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:27.030
want. uh might help the reader and for

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:29.910
example I often will write constants in

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:32.630
uppercase to make it obvious. However,

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:34.870
the language doesn't differentiate if

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:37.990
you change case. Uh it must start with a

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:39.590
letter. Identifiers must start with a

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:41.590
letter, must not end with an underscore

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:43.429
and must not have two underscores in

00:01:43.439 --> 00:01:44.870
them. So those are the rules for

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:46.550
identifiers. And you're not allowed to

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:48.149
use any of the reserved words, of

00:01:48.159 --> 00:01:52.310
course. Uh when choosing uh names of

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:56.149
things, try to be consistent. um uh

00:01:56.159 --> 00:01:58.469
intelligent choices for identifiers make

00:01:58.479 --> 00:02:01.350
your VHDL code much more readable. So

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:03.109
the synthesis tools and simulators don't

00:02:03.119 --> 00:02:05.990
care but code is really written for

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:07.910
other people to be able to read and for

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:09.510
you aren't for you at a later time as

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:11.350
well. So the idea is to make it easy to

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:14.710
read and understand uh and more and more

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:16.309
impressive to co-workers and superiors

00:02:16.319 --> 00:02:17.510
that says actually this is about

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:19.990
communication skills. So the basic data

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:22.550
types we have

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:25.270
um in VHDL are signals and that's mainly

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:27.190
what we're going to be using uh in this

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:30.309
course uh with and constants. So we can

00:02:30.319 --> 00:02:32.390
declare constant values. We have

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:33.750
variables as you have in other

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:35.990
languages. However, their behavior is a

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:38.229
little more complicated and I suggest we

00:02:38.239 --> 00:02:39.910
don't use those at the beginning. And

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:41.670
then we also have files which we won't

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:44.309
be needing to use in this course. So

00:02:44.319 --> 00:02:46.390
signals data systems are obviously about

