signals and their values.

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:51.670
uh they're also made up of components.

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:53.670
So components are things like gates,

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:55.270
flip-flops which are incon

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:57.509
interconnected by those wires and they

00:02:57.519 --> 00:02:59.830
take transform some input signals into

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:01.589
output signals. That's basically what

00:03:01.599 --> 00:03:04.949
we're doing. And if we think of the the

00:03:04.959 --> 00:03:07.110
sequence of assigned values of a signal

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:10.070
over time, it's a waveform.

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:12.949
Um so when assigned a value and the

00:03:12.959 --> 00:03:14.949
operator we use to assign a value to a

00:03:14.959 --> 00:03:16.869
signal is this less than or equal than

00:03:16.879 --> 00:03:19.110
symbol.

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:21.110
uh if it's in a process.

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:23.509
So we have to be careful here. So um if

00:03:23.519 --> 00:03:25.270
you do an assign signal assignment in a

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:27.670
process, the signal is not updated until

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:30.710
the process suspends or terminates.

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:32.309
We'll see what processes are later.

00:03:32.319 --> 00:03:34.070
However, if you do it in data flow,

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:35.910
which is what I'm going to mention here,

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:37.670
the assignment is just like the same as

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:39.830
wiring things. It's immediate. So I

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:41.430
mention this because it's a it's a

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:43.190
common thing that people misunderstand

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:45.750
